Okay, I did a quick debug in MESS of the two versions of Sea Devil.  It took no time at all (it has taken longer to write this email than to check the code).  I'm pretty sure that the two bytes in the homemade cart are bad.  The Z80 actually does try to execute the bytes (as I can tell from the Program Counter when the debugger reaches the watchpoint), so the bytes are NOT graphics.  Here is what it works out to:

Sea Devil (Ward's dump of my cart from 2001)
---------
$2282: $36 $12 - Different Bytes from "Sea Devil" (Homemade cart)

Here is what that means:

$2281: $DD $36 $12 $00    LD (IX+$12),$00

That's legitimate code.



Sea Devil (Homemade Cart)
-------------------------
$2282: $87 $2F - Different Bytes from "Sea Devil"

$2281: $DD $87           DB $DD $87
$2283: $2F               CPL
$2284: $00               NOP

$DD $87 is NOT legitimate code.  However, by byte $2285 of the cart, the code gets back on track.  I'm not sure what the code is supposed to be doing here, but obviously the change is so imperceptible that it doesn't crash the program, nor do we notice any differences when the program runs.

This does bring up a question though.  Normally when the Z80 tries to execute code from a graphics area the program crashes and goes off to never-never land.  When the Z80 encounters:

$2281: $DD $87           DB $DD $87

There is NO opcode for that $DD87.  The closest opcode is $DD86, which is the beginning of:

ADD A, (IX,d)

However, a CLOSE opcode is is not close enough.  What does the Z80 do in this situation?  Does it just skips these bytes and go to the next bytes that it can "understand."  How come it doesn't crash here?

Finally, in my opinion, the homemade cart has lost those two bytes at $2282 and $2283.  When bytes start to go bad on an EPROM it is typical for the bytes to eventually turn to $FF (1111 1111 binary).  The probably bad bytes on the homemade cart are:

$87 = 1111 0111 binary
$2F = 0010 1111 binary

So, it would seem those bytes have bits that are turning from 0 to 1 and the whole byte is on its way to becoming $FF.

Any differing and/or confirming opinions are welcome.

Adam


